# `__modname__` business module

`weld add module __modname__` generated this package. weld writes it once and
records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your edits
survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

An HTTP business module wired through `internal/httpx`:

- `dto.go` — the typed `Request`, `Response`, `CreateRequest` and `Item`, plus
  `CreateRequest.Validate`.
- `service.go` — the `Service` interface, the default `NewService`, and the
  `NewDemoService` demo constructor the package's tests use.
- `module.go` — `Register`: the route table, one statement per route, with each
  handler named rather than inlined.
- `handler.go` — the handlers themselves, one function per route.
- `module_test.go` — an in-process `httptest` suite with the demo service.

## Where code goes

The module follows the same split as the rest of the project: a file that wires
things together stays readable because the work lives elsewhere.

- `module.go` declares routes and nothing else. A route reads as a goal —
  `httpx.Get(router, "/greet", handleGreet(service))` — so the module's surface
  is legible and a handler has a name in a stack trace.
- `handler.go` holds the handler bodies. A handler that needs the `Service` is a
  small constructor over it (`handleGreet(service) httpx.Handler[Request,
  Response]`); one that needs nothing is a bare function (`handleHealth`).
- `dto.go` and `params.go` hold the wire types, and a request type's `Validate`
  holds its rules, so no handler repeats a manual check.
- `service.go` holds the business logic and the dependencies it needs.

Keep the handler bodies out of `Register`: a route declared with an inline
closure hides the surface and makes the logic untestable on its own.

## Routes

`Register` declares every route in one statement, the handler named rather than
inlined (the bodies are in `handler.go`), and paths are relative to the mount the
composition root gives it:

- `GET {prefix}/` — `httpx.NoInput`, a public route with no parameters.
- `GET {prefix}/greet?name=Ada` — `httpx.Get`, which binds the query into
  `Request`.
- `POST {prefix}/admin/items` — `httpx.Post` with `httpx.Policy(adminPolicy)`.
  The named policy rejects an unauthenticated caller before the handler, the
  JSON body is bound, and `CreateRequest.Validate` runs before the handler.
- `GET {prefix}/health` — `router.HandleFunc`, the raw escape hatch for a
  response that must stay unwrapped.

The module declares no mount prefix and no guard. `internal/httpserver.ModulePrefix`
returns the module's prefix (the versioned `APIPrefix/__modname__` by default) and
the generated server graph mounts the module there, so the same module serves
`/api/v1/__modname__` and `/api/v2/__modname__` without an edit; a module that owns
its full paths is mounted at the root by returning `""`. `adminPolicy` is only a name: the composition root defines what the
`"admin"` policy does (`httpserver.AdminGuard` by default), so authorization
lives in exactly one place and every module shares it.

## Authentication and authorization

`adminPolicy` is a name, not a check. At startup the composition root resolves
every referenced name into middleware through `httpx.Router.WithPolicy`, so the
`"admin"` policy is defined once, in `internal/httpserver/policy.go`
(`AdminGuard`), and shared by every module; the server graph mounts it in
`internal/di/di.go`.

The default `AdminGuard` is a placeholder: it only requires a non-empty bearer
token and authenticates nobody. Replace its body with the project's real check.
A guard is an ordinary `httpx.Middleware`, so it runs before the handler and
before the JSON body is bound, and it may close over an injected dependency:
after `weld add db`, pass the repository at the `WithPolicy` call site
(`WithPolicy(httpserver.PolicyAdmin, httpserver.AdminGuard(repository))`) and
look the caller's role up there. On success it puts the caller's identity on
`r.Context()`, which the handler reads from its `ctx`; on failure it writes
`httpx.Error(w, http.StatusForbidden, ...)` and returns.

A name the composition root never defined panics at startup, so a route is never
served unguarded because of a typo. Under a mount that declares a baseline with
`httpx.Router.Require`, a route opts out explicitly with `httpx.Open()`.

## Responses

Handlers write through `internal/httpx`, the shared toolkit:

- `httpx.JSON` wraps a value in the `{code, msg, data}` envelope, with `code`
  repeating the HTTP transport status.
- `httpx.Error` writes an error envelope with `data: null`; a handler can also
  return an `*httpx.HTTPError` such as `httpx.NotFound("...")` and the helper
  maps it.
- `httpx.RawText`, `httpx.Bytes` and `httpx.RawJSON` write a response that must
  stay unwrapped.

## Replaceable example, not persistence

The default `Service` is an in-memory development demo. It writes nothing to
disk and keeps no state across restarts. Replace the body of `NewService` (or
return your own implementation) to add real behaviour. Keep the `Service`
interface and the `NewService` symbol stable.

## How it is wired

Generating this module installed `http` and `loom` (and the `config` they
require). The generated Loom server graph in `internal/di` provides
`__modname__.NewService`, mounts the module under the API prefix
(`httpserver.ModulePrefix`) and defines the `admin` policy once with
`httpserver.AdminGuard`. You may change `NewService`'s signature — for example to
take `data.Repository` after `weld add db` — and the graph follows it. Nothing in
this package quotes the graph, and no separate route file is written.

Adding the optional Cobra command group (`weld add module __modname__ --command`)
reuses the same `Service`, so the command and the HTTP handler share one
business layer.
